WEBVTT

00:00:01.106 --> 00:00:03.167
Hello, my name is Heriyanto.

00:00:03.447 --> 00:00:08.909
I will walk through the trial-class booking solution and explain the main decisions,

00:00:09.229 --> 00:00:14.136
especially what happens when two parents compete for the last seat.

00:00:14.458 --> 00:00:21.062
The app was built using React and Hono at the backend,

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:24.622
and we use SQLite for the DB.

00:00:25.245 --> 00:00:28.865
Why? Because that keeps the project very small,

00:00:28.865 --> 00:00:32.333
with the booking rules in one service

00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:35.953
and the important constraints in the database.

00:00:37.284 --> 00:00:42.952
A parent can choose a child, book a trial, and simulate the payment.

00:00:42.952 --> 00:00:48.630
The teacher dashboard will show the confirmed children only,

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.411
where each class has four places.

00:00:52.411 --> 00:00:57.833
The screen recording uses synthetic families,

00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:01.313
and all the payments are mocked.

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:07.706
OK, here, creating a booking will give a pending-payment status.

00:01:08.173 --> 00:01:09.786
It doesn't reserve a seat,

00:01:09.786 --> 00:01:16.360
and I chose to allocate the seat when a successful payment is processed.

00:01:16.626 --> 00:01:20.802
If payment fails, the child stays off this roster,

00:01:20.802 --> 00:01:23.822
and the same booking can be retried.

00:01:24.102 --> 00:01:28.982
A successful retry will check availability again before confirming.

00:01:31.214 --> 00:01:35.953
Reopening this child and class returns the existing booking.

00:01:36.313 --> 00:01:41.106
The database enforces one booking per child and class.

00:01:41.106 --> 00:01:44.570
Payment attempts are separate records,

00:01:45.373 --> 00:01:51.567
so a failed attempt remains in the history even after the booking becomes confirmed.

00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:55.225
This class starts with three confirmed children,

00:01:55.905 --> 00:01:57.901
so one seat remains.

00:01:58.521 --> 00:02:04.437
Parent A opens a booking first but doesn't pay.

00:02:04.677 --> 00:02:07.277
Parent B then opens another booking

00:02:07.277 --> 00:02:11.253
and completes the successful mock payment first.

00:02:11.537 --> 00:02:13.803
B's child gets the final seat.

00:02:13.803 --> 00:02:18.756
When A's successful payment is processed, the class is already full.

00:02:18.756 --> 00:02:21.566
The result is refund required,

00:02:21.566 --> 00:02:26.408
and A's child is excluded from the roster.

00:02:26.408 --> 00:02:30.691
The roster still contains exactly four children.

00:02:30.691 --> 00:02:36.491
I chose this outcome because payment success and enrollment success are separate facts.

00:02:37.188 --> 00:02:42.108
Recording a refund obligation preserves that distinction.

00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:46.632
It does not claim that a refund has been issued.

00:02:47.152 --> 00:02:49.912
A temporary seat hold is another option,

00:02:49.912 --> 00:02:54.512
but would need expiry and late-payment rules.

00:02:55.429 --> 00:02:58.889
The critical detail is where the capacity check happens.

00:02:59.229 --> 00:03:04.109
Locally, the service starts an immediate SQLite write transaction

00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:07.209
before reading the number of confirmed bookings.

00:03:07.914 --> 00:03:11.354
SQLite allows one writer at a time.

00:03:11.674 --> 00:03:14.494
If B gets the transaction first,

00:03:14.734 --> 00:03:18.694
B can read three confirmed bookings and confirm the fourth.

00:03:18.694 --> 00:03:23.221
A must wait to acquire its write transaction.

00:03:23.221 --> 00:03:25.958
After B commits, A reads four,

00:03:25.958 --> 00:03:28.778
so A cannot also take that seat.

00:03:28.778 --> 00:03:33.849
If the database remains busy, the API returns a retryable error.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:40.509
The booking update and payment-attempt insert are inside the same transaction.

00:03:41.228 --> 00:03:47.090
They commit together, or both roll back if an operation fails.

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:54.379
Capacity triggers also reject a fifth confirmed booking on insert or update.

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:58.659
That protects against a future writer bypassing the service.

00:03:58.999 --> 00:04:04.399
Disabling a button in React shouldn't protect against another browser or process.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:10.260
Another failure case is losing the response after the server commits.

00:04:10.558 --> 00:04:15.379
The browser cannot assume that means the payment failed.

00:04:15.744 --> 00:04:19.775
Before sending the request, it saves a unique payment key

00:04:19.775 --> 00:04:24.303
and the intended outcome in session storage.

00:04:24.303 --> 00:04:27.823
If the response is uncertain, it keeps that key.

00:04:28.023 --> 00:04:32.103
Retrying the same command returns the original attempt

00:04:32.103 --> 00:04:34.503
without recording another one.

00:04:35.179 --> 00:04:38.079
Using the key with different input returns a conflict.

00:04:38.439 --> 00:04:42.219
The replay check happens before rejecting a final booking,

00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:45.239
so a lost confirmation response can still be recovered.

00:04:45.239 --> 00:04:48.476
This solves repeated commands.

00:04:48.476 --> 00:04:53.208
The transaction handles competition for capacity.

00:04:53.208 --> 00:04:55.008
They solve different problems.

00:04:55.983 --> 00:05:00.429
The concurrency test starts eight independent processes,

00:05:00.429 --> 00:05:05.447
each with its own connection to the same SQLite database.

00:05:05.691 --> 00:05:09.130
They wait behind a readiness barrier,

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:12.067
and then compete for one remaining seat.

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:18.528
The test checks one confirmation, seven refund obligations, and a roster of four.

00:05:18.828 --> 00:05:23.288
Another test sends five concurrent copies of one payment key,

00:05:23.288 --> 00:05:26.828
and checks that only one attempt is stored.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:33.270
There are also tests for an insert failure rolling back the booking update,

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:37.950
full and started classes, duplicate bookings, and ownership checks.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.712
These exercise the important failure paths,

00:05:41.712 --> 00:05:45.712
although they aren't a production load benchmark.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:51.039
The hosted version uses a Cloudflare Worker for the frontend

00:05:51.039 --> 00:05:57.856
and routes API requests to one shared SQLite backend called a Durable Object.

00:05:58.056 --> 00:06:01.296
It runs the same booking service and schema.

00:06:01.776 --> 00:06:04.474
The storage mechanism is different from Bun.

00:06:04.674 --> 00:06:09.334
The adapter uses Cloudflare's native synchronous transaction API

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:14.094
to keep the database operations together and roll them back on an exception.

00:06:14.608 --> 00:06:18.890
All parents share that object because they compete

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:22.489
for the same class capacity.

00:06:22.829 --> 00:06:27.629
One database per parent would break that coordination.

00:06:28.189 --> 00:06:33.856
A single shared object is simple for the demo, but also limits write throughput.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:41.805
For real use, I would add trusted authentication and verified payment-provider events,

00:06:41.805 --> 00:06:45.400
like using Google auth,

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.040
and then we put the webhook to verify the payment

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.821
when the payment returns success from a provider like Midtrans

00:06:54.821 --> 00:07:00.296
and others, including amount and currency checks.

00:07:00.621 --> 00:07:03.443
Refunds would need execution and reconciliation.

00:07:03.963 --> 00:07:09.827
Provider network calls should stay outside the short database transaction.

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:14.647
I used Codex extensively for the implementation and testing,

00:07:14.647 --> 00:07:19.694
and directed the product and delivery choices.

00:07:20.034 --> 00:07:25.554
The repository documents that assistance under the AI guidance.

00:07:25.906 --> 00:07:29.725
The main decisions are to confirm seats automatically,

00:07:30.265 --> 00:07:31.625
preserve payment history,

00:07:31.625 --> 00:07:35.445
and make uncertain requests safe to retry.

00:07:35.625 --> 00:07:38.505
That keeps the roster correct while making unsuccessful

00:07:38.505 --> 00:07:40.730
enrollment explicit,

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.706
and prevents more complications.

00:07:46.146 --> 00:07:48.546
Thank you for reviewing my solution.
